Apache Maven is a powerful build automation and project management tool used primarily for Java-based projects.
It is designed to simplify the build process and standardize how software projects are structured and managed.

Key Functions of Maven:
Build Automation: Maven automates the creation of software builds, handling tasks like compiling source code, running tests, 
and packaging code into distributable formats (like JAR or WAR files).

Dependency Management: This is one of Maven's most significant features. 
Instead of manually downloading external libraries (JAR files) and adding them to your project, you define them in a configuration file. 
Maven then automatically fetches these libraries from a central repository and manages their versions, ensuring your project has everything it needs to run.

Project Object Model (POM): Maven uses a file called pom.xml. This file acts as the central configuration for your project,
defining its dependencies, build plugins, and project details in a standardized XML format.

Standardized Lifecycle: Maven enforces a consistent build lifecycle, which includes standard phases like validate, compile, test, package, install, and deploy. 
This makes it easier for developers to understand and work on any Maven-based project, regardless of who created it.
In essence, Maven removes much of the manual, repetitive work involved in software development, acting as an "engine room" that keeps your project's build process 
consistent and efficient.

============================
Maven provides a standard build lifecycle. Here are the common commands used to manage Java and Spring Boot projects:

mvn clean : Removes the target folder, effectively deleting all previously compiled files and build artifacts to ensure a fresh start.
mvn validate : Checks that the project is correct and all necessary information is available in the pom.xml file.
mvn compile : Compiles the source code of the project into the target folder.
mvn test : Runs the unit tests for the application using a suitable testing framework.
mvn package : Compiles the code and packages it into a distributable format, such as a JAR (Java ARchive) file, which you can then run or share.
mvn install : Installs the packaged JAR file into your local .m2 repository (~/.m2/repository), making it available for other projects on your machine to use as a dependency.
Note on Maven Wrapper: If Maven is not installed globally on your system, you can use the Maven Wrapper (provided with Spring Boot projects) by replacing mvn with ./mvnw (on Linux/macOS) or mvnw.cmd (on Windows), for example: ./mvnw package.
